one of the key criteria to being  successful in investing is to always  learn from the past and as we saw in the  2008 2009 financial crisis the housing  market fell off a cliff but if we  actually go back and we look as to why  this occurred it's actually quite  compelling as you can see here in this  chart back in 2008 2009 that red line  shows that housing was exploding upwards  in value and all of a sudden starting in  about 2004 the federal reserve decided  to raise interest rates the federal  funds effective rate went through the  roof going up over four and a half  percent and what followed very closely  after was that housing prices collapsed  you continue to see that red line trend  downwards now if you look all the way to  the far right you can see that housing  prices have been exploding upwards and  in that blue line you see that the  federal fund's effective rate is  starting to creep upwards now coming  close to 1.5 percent as we see the  federal funds rate continue to climb  higher and higher it increases the odds  that there could be some sort of housing  collapse now just because in the past  housing prices went up and then federal  fund rates went up meant that it crashed  that doesn't mean that it's going to  happen in the future but what we do have  to be careful of is that u.s housing is  definitely in the danger zone the odds  are increasing that there could be a  housing market collapse what you need to  understand is how to be prepared in a  bull or bear scenario housing prices  going to the moon are definitely a  function of loose monetary policy and so  much money sloshing around an economy  looking for a place to be stored to  protect themselves from that nine plus  percent inflation that is being  experienced in the united states but if  the federal reserve is going to continue  to aggressively raise rates and they are  going to conduct quantitative tightening  naturally housing demand is going to  fall and if housing demand falls  prices usually follow keep your head on  a swivel stay educated and make sure  you're prepared housing prices are the  highest they've ever been in the united  states both for brand new homes and the  average american home so naturally  there's a reversion to the mean  which means you just need to be prepared  to be able to weather the bad times
 

